      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Spinal cord stimulation (SCS) has been used in the treatment of severe angina pectoris since the 1980s. Several studies have shown both an antianginal and an anti-ischaemic effect. There are several theories about the mechanism behind the anti-ischaemic effect of SCS, including the possibility that it is dependent on an increase in coronary flow velocity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine if there were effects of SCS on coronary flow velocity during cardiac stress."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Eight patients with severe anginal pain secondary to coronary artery disease who had been implanted with an SCS device were included in the study. In addition, four patients with syndrome X were examined. If possible, a Doppler guidewire was placed in the vessel corresponding to the ischaemic area revealed on a prior myocardial scintigram. A temporary pacemaker electrode was placed in the right atrium. Atrial pacing started at 80 beats/min and increased by 10 beats/min every 2 min until the patient experienced moderate angina; the pacing frequency was then maintained at the same level. After 2 min of pacing at this frequency, SCS treatment commenced; after a further 5 min, pacing was stopped. Throughout the procedure, coronary flow velocity, assessed as average peak velocity (APV), was monitored continually."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "APV increased during pacing in all the patients with coronary artery disease (mean increase 53%; P < 0.02). There were no significant changes in APV during maximum pacing frequency when stimulation was introduced."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The results of this study do not support the theory that the anti-ischaemic effect of SCS is dependent on an increase in coronary flow velocity."
        }
      ]},
